damentals": {"margin": 8.55, "lean": 4.5, "environment": 4.05, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 14.83, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 6.88, "baseline_margin": 12.32, "analyst_adjustment": 0.18, "key_factors": ["Colorado's 6-point Democratic lean in an open-seat race", "All six expert raters call it Solid/Safe D", "Prediction markets price Democrats at 97%", "Marx's fundraising burn rate and unusual headlines limit his appeal"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Democratic margin in the low-to-mid teens in a state that leans six points blue, and every expert rater plus all three prediction markets agree the Democrat is a heavy favorite. With no public polling, the forecast stays near the fundamentals-based estimate rather than stretching further.", "watch": "A late surge of unified Republican consolidation behind Marx, or a polling error in off-year open-seat races, could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Jared Polis (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Colorado's governorship is open as Jared Polis is term-limited, leaving Attorney General Phil Weiser as the Democratic nominee against Republican Victor Marx. The state leans Democratic by about six points, and the race has drawn little polling, so the picture rests on fundamentals, expert ratings, and markets. All six major raters place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets on Kalshi, Polymarket, and PredictIt each price the Democrat near 97 percent.\n\nMarx has kept pace with Weiser in fundraising but is spending it quickly, and headlines about a skipped debate and an exorcism claim have drawn unfavorable attention. An independent, Greg Lopez, is also on the ballot and debated Weiser in Marx's absence. The expected Democratic margin sits in the low teens, with meaningful uncertainty given the absence of polls.", "history": [{"dat